{%- comment -%}
  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ LOCKED — the pink gummy scroll sequence. Signed off 11 Sep 2026.          │
  │                                                                           │
  │ .gh-stage heights, .gh-pin's --zoom-k / --rise-end, .gh-pin .gh-gummy and │
  │ .gh-reveal's transform and fade are approved as they stand. Do not tune   │
  │ them while fixing something nearby.                                       │
  │                                                                           │
  │ tests/locked-hero.test.mjs asserts every one of those values and fails    │
  │ loudly if any changes — that is the flag, for either of us. Changing one  │
  │ on purpose means updating that file in the same commit and saying in the  │
  │ message that the lock was lifted.                                         │
  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%} GummyChums homepage — coming-soon vibe, pink-forward, scroll fall-in kept {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign s = section.settings -%}
<style>
  /* No negative margin: it made this element wider than the viewport, which iOS
     turns into a horizontal scroll. Sections handle their own gutters. */
  .gc-home{overflow-x:clip;color:var(--c-ink)}
  .gc-home *{box-sizing:border-box}
  .gc-wrap{max-width:var(--container);margin-inline:auto;padding-inline:var(--gutter)}

  /* ---------- ACT 1 — hero + scroll fall-in ---------- */
  @property --z{syntax:"<number>";inherits:true;initial-value:0}
  @property --gh-ground{syntax:"<length>";inherits:true;initial-value:0px}
  .gh-stage{position:relative;height:270vh}
  /* Both sequences run as scroll-driven animations, not scroll handlers. The
     handlers were never slow — measured on the real page in Safari, a --z write
     plus a full style flush costs 0.4ms over baseline. They are LATE: a handler
     runs behind the compositor, so through a momentum flick the pinned content
     chases the finger and corrects, which is the back-and-forth.

     Ranges are absolute scroll offsets in px, published once by the script from
     layout. Deliberately NOT view()/contain: those resolve against the
     scrollport, and iOS resizes the scrollport every time its toolbar collapses,
     which re-scales the timeline mid-scroll and makes everything on it jump —
     worse than the handler it replaces. A scroll offset in px cannot move when
     the toolbar does. */
  @supports (animation-timeline:scroll()){
    .gh-stage{animation:ghZ linear both, ghGround linear both;
      animation-timeline:scroll(root block), scroll(root block);
      animation-range:var(--gh-z0,0px) var(--gh-z1,0px), 0px var(--gh-lift,0px)}
    @keyframes ghZ{from{--z:0}to{--z:1}}
    @keyframes ghGround{0%{--gh-ground:calc(var(--gh-pad,0px) + var(--gh-lift,0px))}
      100%{--gh-ground:var(--gh-pad,0px)}}
  }
  /* Shorter pin on phones. With no scrollbar there is no feedback that the page
     is still scrolling, so a long pin reads as a frozen page. 120vh leaves
     ~160px of pinned scroll — enough for the effect, short enough not to alarm. */
  /* 160vh, not 120. The pin is 100svh, so the stage height MINUS one viewport is
     the entire scroll budget the zoom and the reveal have to share. At 120vh
     that was 20vh — about 160px, one flick — and with the reveal only starting
     at z=0.60 the words "WHAT'S INSIDE?" were legible for the last ~22px of it.
     Unreadable, which is exactly the complaint.
     160vh gives 60vh (~490px) instead. Still well short of the 200vh that once
     read as a frozen page, and unlike that version something is moving the
     whole way down: the gummy grows, then the words arrive and HOLD. */
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-stage{height:270vh}}
  /* transparent so the global fixed flavour background shows through — nothing to repaint */
  .gh-pin{--zoom-k:26;--rev-start:.62;--rev-ramp:3.6;position:sticky;top:0;height:100svh;overflow:hidden;display:grid;place-items:center;
    background:transparent;contain:paint;padding-bottom:4vh}

  /* The zooming gummy. Scaling is anchored to the CIRCLE's own centre
     (cy=58 in a 120 viewBox = 48.333%), not the box, and the element rides up
     from below the fold to dead-centre as --z grows. That combination is what
     guarantees full coverage at any aspect ratio: the old `center bottom`
     origin sat 14% below the fold, so on a tall phone the circle climbed out
     of the top before it ever reached the bottom edge — leaving a ~74px strip
     of background along the bottom at peak zoom. Scaling harder made it worse,
     since the circle rises faster than it grows. */
  /* A CSS circle, not the gc-gummy SVG. This element scales to 21x — measured
     at 1733x1733 CSS px, which on a phone at DPR 3 is ~27 million device pixels.
     Re-rasterising an SVG document (four radial gradients, six shapes, a rotate)
     at that size is the most phone-hostile thing on the page; a single CSS
     gradient on one rounded box is a far cheaper raster. Nothing is lost: past
     about 4x the highlights are off-screen and it reads as a flat colour wash.
     Origin is 50% now the shape fills its box — the SVG's circle sat at 48.333%. */
  /* Dawn's base.css carries `a:empty, div:empty, p:empty, … {display:none}` as a
     guard against stray markup, and it beats a bare class: `div:empty` scores
     0,1,1 and `.gh-gummy` only 0,1,0. This element is DELIBERATELY empty — the
     whole gummy is one CSS gradient now — so the selector is qualified to clear
     that bar. Same reason the reduced-motion rule below is qualified: it has to
     out-specify this one in turn.

     TIMING. The point of this element is that it fills the screen and the words
     land ON it. It used to reach full coverage around z=0.78 while the reveal was
     already fully opaque at 0.66 — so the text arrived over the cream background
     with a pink ball still climbing underneath it. The three phases are now
     ordered properly and each is given room:

       0    → 0.22   hero copy fades out
       0.22 → 0.30   gummy finishes rising and covers the screen
       0.32 → 0.46   WHAT'S INSIDE? fades in, on solid pink
       0.46 → 1.00   held there

     Percentages are the wrong unit to judge this in — what a thumb feels is
     DISTANCE. Measured on a phone, against the theme this replaced:

                        old theme      first attempt      now
       pin length         162px            487px         244px
       text starts         97px            234px          78px
       fully legible      140px            302px         112px
       held legible        22px            185px         132px

     The first attempt fixed the flash-past by making everything longer, which
     just moved the wait to the front. Compressing the phases instead gets the
     words on screen sooner than the old theme managed AND keeps them there six
     times longer — while still landing them on solid pink.

     Sized in vmax and capped rather than scaled without limit. Coverage needs a
     circle as wide as the viewport DIAGONAL, and the diagonal-to-vmax ratio is
     worst on a square-ish window: 1.414, against 1.0 for a very tall or very
     wide one. A cap of 13 clears that by barely 1%, measured at ONE pixel of
     margin on a 444x458 window — so it is 14, which leaves ~9%. The cap only
     bounds the size; when coverage happens is set by the 140 multiplier, and
     that puts it at z=0.25 on a phone and z=0.29 on a square window, both
     before the words start at 0.32.

     Capping also stops the raster growing forever. Peak is ~1,157px on a phone
     where it used to be 1,733 — under half the pixels to rasterise. */
  .gh-pin .gh-gummy{display:block;position:absolute;left:50%;top:auto;bottom:4%;width:min(32vw,190px);aspect-ratio:1;z-index:1;
    border-radius:50%;backface-visibility:hidden;transform-origin:center bottom;
    background:radial-gradient(circle at 36% 30%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-cherry) 50%, white) 0%,
      var(--c-cherry) 52%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-cherry) 76%, black) 100%);
    opacity:1;
    transform:translateX(-50%) scale(calc(1 + var(--z,0) * var(--z,0) * var(--zoom-k)))}
  /* will-change only while the hero is actually on screen — a permanent one
     pins a layer in GPU memory for the whole page */

  .gh-stage.gh-live .gh-gummy{will-change:transform,opacity}
  /* ---- THE CHUMS · the real characters from Brand Book v2 ----
     They stand on the hero's bottom edge and flank the centre column, which is
     how the book's own cover is composed. Anchored bottom-left / bottom-right
     rather than centred, so they stay planted while the viewport changes shape.

     They fade on the same --z curve as .gh-mid, so the scroll-zoom hands over
     to the reveal with nothing left floating over it.

     The sway rotates about 50% 100% — the feet. Rotating about the centre made
     them look like they were hovering rather than shifting their weight.

     --gh-ground, not bottom:0. The pin is 100svh tall but starts BELOW the
     marquee and header, so at scroll 0 its bottom edge sits ~129px under the
     fold and the figures were cut off at the knees — which is the only moment
     they're at full opacity, since they fade out within 60px of scrolling.
     The script keeps --gh-ground equal to however far the pin overhangs the
     fold, so their feet stay on the fold at rest and settle onto the pin's own
     bottom edge once it's pinned. */
  .gcg{width:100%;height:auto;display:block}
  .gh-chum{position:absolute;bottom:var(--gh-ground,0px);z-index:1;pointer-events:none;line-height:0;
    opacity:calc(1 - var(--z,0) * 2.6);transform-origin:50% 100%;
    animation:ghSway ease-in-out infinite alternate}
  .gh-chum img{display:block;width:auto;height:100%;
    -webkit-user-drag:none;user-select:none;-webkit-touch-callout:none}
  .gh-chum--l{left:0;height:min(56vh,500px);animation-duration:6.5s}
  .gh-chum--r{right:0;height:min(52vh,466px);animation-duration:8.2s;animation-delay:-3s}
  @keyframes ghSway{from{transform:translate3d(0,0,0) rotate(-1.2deg)}to{transform:translate3d(0,-8px,0) rotate(1.2deg)}}

  /* Below ~1100px the centre column and the figures start sharing horizontal
     space, so the figures move outward and shrink rather than overlap the copy.
     On phones they bleed roughly half off each edge: enough that you read them
     as two people standing there, not enough to crowd the headline. */
  @media(max-width:1100px){.gh-chum--l{height:min(42vh,360px);left:8px}}
  @media(max-width:1100px){.gh-chum--r{height:min(39vh,340px);right:8px}}
  /* On a 375px screen the copy column and two standing figures cannot both
     have the width they want. Rather than shrink the Chums to slivers, they go
     BIGGER and bleed further out: about a third of each figure is visible, which
     still reads unmistakably as a person. Where the subline crosses them it
     gets a cream halo, so the overlap looks composed instead of accidental. */
  /* NOTHING BLEEDS OFF THE EDGE. Every figure sits fully inside the viewport at
     every width — a Chum cut in half by the screen edge reads as a broken page
     rather than as a crop, and that is worth more than the size bleeding bought.

     Height is capped by BOTH dimensions, which is the part that took two passes
     to get right. The svh term keeps the figures clear of the copy stacked above
     them; the vw term keeps roughly 200px open between them, because that is what
     the "Shop the flavours" button needs and at 360px wide the figures were
     otherwise sitting under it by 25px a side. The floor keeps them recognisable
     on the shortest phone, the ceiling stops them dominating the tallest. */
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-chum--l{height:clamp(150px,min(calc(100svh - 520px),calc((100vw - 200px) * 1.31)),300px);left:6px}}
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-chum--r{height:clamp(142px,min(calc(100svh - 536px),calc((100vw - 200px) * 1.24)),284px);right:6px}}
  /* the shortest phones have no room for a scroll hint under the button once
     the figures are in; the gradient and the Chums already say there is more */
  @media(max-width:749px) and (max-height:700px){.gh-scroll{display:none}}
  /* Narrowed so it wraps INSIDE the gap between the two figures rather than
     running under their arms. In px, not ch: this typeface's "0" is ~19.6px
     wide at this size, so 20ch computed to 393px — wider than the phone. The
     measured gap between the figures is 228px. The halo stays as insurance
     for 320px screens, where the gap closes further. */
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-mid .gh-sub{max-width:280px}}
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-mid .gh-cta2{text-shadow:0 0 10px var(--c-bg),0 0 22px var(--c-bg)}}


  .gh-mid{position:relative;z-index:2;text-align:center;padding:0 var(--sp-5);
    opacity:calc(1 - var(--z,0) * 2.6);will-change:opacity}
  .gh-eyebrow{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:var(--sp-2);background:var(--c-ink);color:var(--c-surface);
    font-weight:var(--fw-black);font-size:var(--fs-xs);letter-spacing:var(--ls-wide);text-transform:uppercase;
    padding:var(--sp-2) var(--sp-4);border-radius:var(--r-full);margin-bottom:var(--sp-4)}
  .gh-tag mark{background:linear-gradient(180deg,transparent 58%,var(--c-accent-soft) 58%);
    color:inherit;padding:0 .06em}
  .gh-cta2{color:var(--c-ink);font-weight:var(--fw-black);font-size:var(--fs-base);text-decoration:none;
    display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:var(--sp-2);padding:var(--sp-1) var(--sp-1);border-bottom:2.5px solid var(--c-ink)}
  .gh-cta2 span{transition:transform var(--dur-2) var(--ease);display:inline-block}
  .gh-cta2:hover span{transform:translateX(5px)}
  .gh-logo{display:block;width:min(470px,74vw);height:auto;margin:0 auto var(--sp-5);
    -webkit-user-drag:none;user-select:none;-webkit-touch-callout:none;pointer-events:none}
  .gh-tag{font-size:var(--fs-3xl);font-weight:var(--fw-black);letter-spacing:var(--ls-tight);line-height:var(--lh-tight);margin:0 auto;max-width:17ch}
  .gh-sub{font-size:var(--fs-lg);font-weight:var(--fw-body);color:var(--c-ink-2);margin:var(--sp-3) auto 0;max-width:34ch;line-height:var(--lh-body)}
  .gh-cta{margin-top:var(--sp-5);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:var(--sp-4);flex-wrap:wrap}
  .gh-pills{display:flex;gap:var(--sp-2);justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:var(--sp-4)}
  .gh-pill{background:var(--c-surface);border-radius:var(--r-full);padding:var(--sp-2) var(--sp-4);font-size:var(--fs-sm);font-weight:var(--fw-black);
    letter-spacing:var(--ls-normal);box-shadow:var(--sh-2);outline:1px solid var(--c-line);outline-offset:-1px}
  .gh-scroll{margin-top:var(--sp-4);font-weight:var(--fw-black);letter-spacing:var(--ls-wide);font-size:var(--fs-xs);color:var(--c-ink-3);
    animation:ghBounce 1.7s ease-in-out infinite}
  @keyframes ghBounce{0%,100%{transform:translateY(0)}50%{transform:translateY(7px)}}
  .gh-reveal{position:absolute;inset:0;
    z-index:3;display:grid;place-items:center;text-align:center;padding:0 var(--sp-5);
    color:var(--c-surface);opacity:calc((var(--z,0) - var(--rev-start)) * var(--rev-ramp));
    pointer-events:none;transform:none;
    pointer-events:none}
  /* Dawn's base.css sets an explicit colour on h1-h6, and a direct rule beats
     the colour inherited from .gh-reveal — so headings on dark/coloured
     backgrounds must restate it or they render near-black. */
  .gh-reveal h2{font-size:var(--fs-4xl);font-weight:var(--fw-black);letter-spacing:var(--ls-tight);line-height:var(--lh-tight);margin:0;color:var(--c-surface)}

  .gh-reveal p{font-size:var(--fs-xl);font-weight:var(--fw-bold);margin:var(--sp-4) auto 0;max-width:46ch;opacity:.92;text-wrap:balance}

  /* The dissolve sits ON the hero's last 128px rather than below it, which is
     the whole point: the teeth are page-colour and the gaps are the hero's own
     pink, so neither half has a colour to match and there is no seam. Pulled up
     by its exact height, and above the stage in the stacking order. */
  .gh-edge{line-height:0;position:relative;z-index:2;margin-top:-128px;pointer-events:none}

  /* ---------- ACT 2 — flavours ---------- */
  /* the dissolve already supplies the top separation */
  .gh-sec{padding:clamp(var(--sp-7),6vw,var(--sp-8)) 0}
  .gh-head{text-align:center;margin-bottom:clamp(var(--sp-5),4vw,var(--sp-7))}
  .gh-head h2{font-size:var(--fs-3xl);font-weight:var(--fw-black);letter-spacing:var(--ls-tight);line-height:var(--lh-tight);margin:var(--sp-2) 0 0}
  .gh-head p{font-size:var(--fs-lg);font-weight:var(--fw-body);color:var(--c-ink-2);margin:var(--sp-3) auto 0;max-width:40ch}


  /* shorter, balanced media ratio — no more empty 1:1 void */

  /* cluster of 2-3 gummies at different sizes/rotations — reads as product, not an icon */
  .gh-cluster{position:absolute;inset:0}
  .gh-g{position:absolute;display:block;animation:ghBob ease-in-out infinite alternate}
  .gh-g1{width:44%;left:9%;top:19%;transform:rotate(-9deg);animation-duration:6.5s}
  .gh-g2{width:29%;right:9%;top:9%;transform:rotate(13deg);animation-duration:7.8s;animation-delay:-2.4s}
  .gh-g3{width:26%;left:36%;bottom:8%;transform:rotate(-15deg);animation-duration:7.1s;animation-delay:-4.2s}
  @keyframes ghBob{0%{translate:0 0}100%{translate:0 -9px}}

  .gh-priced{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--sp-1)}
  .gh-price{font-size:var(--fs-xl);font-weight:var(--fw-black);line-height:1}
  .gh-perday{font-size:var(--fs-xs);font-weight:var(--fw-bold);color:var(--c-ink-3)}

  /* ---------- meet the Chums: a vertical scroll that rides sideways ----------
     Scrolling down pins this block and slides the track left through the three
     Chums, then lets go and normal scrolling resumes. The section is made
     exactly as tall as the sideways travel, so a pixel of scroll is a pixel of
     movement — 1:1, which is what stops a pin from feeling stuck: something is
     always moving under your thumb, and it ends the moment the last Chum is in.

     Without JavaScript, or with reduced motion, there is no pin at all: the
     row is an ordinary swipeable strip with snap points. The pinned ride is
     layered on top of that, never required by it. */
  .gh-hs{position:relative}
  .gh-hs-pin{display:flex;align-items:center;padding:var(--sp-7) 0}
  .gh-hs--js .gh-hs-pin{position:sticky;top:0;height:100svh;box-sizing:border-box;
    padding:var(--header-h) 0 var(--sp-5);overflow:hidden}
  .gh-hs-track{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:var(--sp-4);padding-inline:var(--gutter);
    overflow-x:auto;scroll-snap-type:x mandatory;scrollbar-width:none}
  .gh-hs--js .gh-hs-track{overflow:visible;scroll-snap-type:none;width:max-content}
  .gh-hs--live .gh-hs-track{will-change:transform}
  .gh-hs-intro{flex:0 0 84vw;scroll-snap-align:start}
  .gh-hs-title{font-size:var(--fs-display);font-weight:var(--fw-black);letter-spacing:var(--ls-tight);
    margin:0;color:var(--c-ink);text-wrap:balance}
  .gh-hs-title em{font-style:normal;color:var(--c-accent)}
  .gh-hs-scroll{display:none}
  @media(min-width:750px){.gh-hs-swipe{display:none}.gh-hs-scroll{display:inline}}
  .gh-hs-hint{margin:var(--sp-5) 0 0;font-size:var(--fs-sm);font-weight:var(--fw-black);
    letter-spacing:var(--ls-wide);text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--c-ink-3)}
  @media(min-width:750px){.gh-hs-intro{flex-basis:min(60vw,880px)}}
  /* One card is now the product card as well: the Chum stands beside the shot,
     then name, price, per-day and Add to bag. "Meet the three" said the same
     thing a second time, so it is gone and this ride sits in its place.

     The shot is the product's featured image the moment one is uploaded in
     Shopify; until then it is the gummy cluster the old grid used. The media
     box has a fixed, clamped height so the Chum's height is definite and it can
     never be cropped — on an iPhone SE2 the whole card is ~470px of a 561px
     pinned area. */
  .gh-hs-card{position:relative;flex:0 0 84vw;scroll-snap-align:start;box-sizing:border-box;background:var(--hs-soft);
    border-radius:var(--r-lg);padding:var(--sp-5);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--sp-3)}
  .gh-hs-badge{align-self:flex-start;background:var(--c-surface);color:var(--hs-tone);font-size:var(--fs-xs);
    font-weight:var(--fw-black);letter-spacing:var(--ls-wide);text-transform:uppercase;
    padding:var(--sp-2) var(--sp-3);border-radius:var(--r-full);box-shadow:var(--sh-1)}
  .gh-hs-media{display:flex;align-items:flex-end;height:clamp(190px,34svh,340px);box-sizing:border-box;
    background:var(--c-surface);border-radius:var(--r-md);padding:0 var(--sp-3)}
  .gh-hs-shot{flex:1 1 auto;min-width:0;align-self:stretch;position:relative;display:flex;
    align-items:center;justify-content:center}
  .gh-hs-photo{display:block;max-width:100%;max-height:100%;width:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain}
  .gh-hs-shot .gh-cluster{position:absolute;inset:0}
  /* overlaps the shot a little, as if presenting it; never leaves the card */
  .gh-hs-fig{flex:0 0 auto;display:block;height:94%;width:auto;position:relative;z-index:0;
    margin-left:calc(var(--sp-5) * -1);-webkit-user-drag:none;user-select:none;-webkit-touch-callout:none}
  .gh-hs-name{font-size:var(--fs-xl);font-weight:var(--fw-black);letter-spacing:var(--ls-tight);
    margin:var(--sp-1) 0 0;color:var(--c-ink);text-wrap:balance}
  .gh-hs-name a{color:inherit;text-decoration:none}
  /* one stretched link per card: the whole card is clickable, but there is still
     exactly one focusable element and no nested anchors */
  .gh-hs-name a::after{content:"";position:absolute;inset:0;z-index:2;border-radius:var(--r-lg)}
  .gh-hs-meta{font-size:var(--fs-xs);font-weight:var(--fw-bold);color:var(--c-ink-3);margin:0;letter-spacing:var(--ls-normal)}
  .gh-hs-cta{flex:0 0 auto;background:var(--hs-tone);color:var(--c-surface);font-size:var(--fs-sm);
    font-weight:var(--fw-black);padding:var(--sp-3) var(--sp-5);border-radius:var(--r-full);
    white-space:nowrap;transition:filter var(--dur-2) var(--ease)}
  .gh-hs-card:hover .gh-hs-cta{filter:brightness(1.08)}
  .gh-hs-cta--out{background:var(--c-ink-3)}
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.gh-hs-cta{transition:none}}
  .gh-hs-line{font-size:var(--fs-sm);font-weight:var(--fw-body);color:var(--c-ink-2);margin:0;line-height:var(--lh-snug)}
  .gh-hs-buy{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:var(--sp-3);margin-top:auto}
  .gh-hs-card .gh-price{color:var(--c-ink)}
  @media(min-width:750px){.gh-hs-card{flex-basis:min(40vw,560px)}}
  /* Phone: a little narrower than the screen, so the next Chum peeks in and you
     can see the row continues, and trailing space equal to the leftover screen
     so the third Chum can ride all the way to where the others sit. Both panels
     must match, or the travel maths in gc-hs.js lands on the wrong card. */
  @media(max-width:749px){
    /* a whole header of empty space above the cards on a 667px screen — the
       header is not overlaying this section, so it does not need clearing */
    .gh-hs--js .gh-hs-pin{padding-top:var(--sp-4);padding-bottom:var(--sp-4)}
    .gh-hs-intro,.gh-hs-card{flex-basis:76vw}
    .gh-hs-track{padding-inline-end:24vw;scroll-padding-inline-start:var(--gutter);
      -webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;overscroll-behavior-x:contain}
    .gh-hs-track::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}
  }
  @supports (animation-timeline:scroll()){
    .gh-hs--js .gh-hs-track{animation:ghRide linear both;animation-timeline:scroll(root block);
      animation-range:var(--gh-r0,0px) var(--gh-r1,0px)}
    @keyframes ghRide{from{transform:translate3d(0,0,0)}
      to{transform:translate3d(calc(-1 * var(--gh-travel,0px)),0,0)}}
  }

  /* ---------- ACT 4 — FAQ ---------- */
  .gh-faq{max-width:760px;margin:0 auto}
  .gh-q{background:var(--c-surface);border-radius:var(--r-md);margin-bottom:var(--sp-3);overflow:hidden;box-shadow:var(--sh-2)}
  .gh-q summary{cursor:pointer;list-style:none;padding:var(--sp-4) var(--sp-5);font-weight:var(--fw-black);font-size:var(--fs-base);
    display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:var(--sp-3)}
  .gh-q summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}
  .gh-q summary::after{content:"+";font-size:var(--fs-xl);color:var(--c-accent);transition:transform var(--dur-3) var(--ease);line-height:1}
  .gh-q[open] summary::after{transform:rotate(45deg)}
  .gh-q p{margin:0;padding:0 var(--sp-5) var(--sp-5);font-weight:var(--fw-body);font-size:var(--fs-base);color:var(--c-ink-2);line-height:var(--lh-body)}
  .gh-q p a{color:var(--c-accent);font-weight:var(--fw-black);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px}

  /* ---------- ACT 5 — closing CTA ---------- */
  .gh-end{text-align:center;padding:clamp(var(--sp-8),8vw,var(--sp-10)) var(--sp-5)}
  .gh-end h2{font-size:var(--fs-3xl);font-weight:var(--fw-black);letter-spacing:var(--ls-tight);line-height:var(--lh-tight);margin:0 auto;max-width:16ch}
  .gh-end h2 em{font-style:normal;color:var(--c-accent)}
  .gh-end p{font-size:var(--fs-lg);font-weight:var(--fw-body);color:var(--c-ink-2);margin:var(--sp-4) auto var(--sp-6);max-width:36ch}

  /* ---------- phone: room to breathe ----------
     Deliberately last in this stylesheet. These override base rules declared
     above, and at equal specificity the later rule wins — anywhere earlier and
     they silently do nothing.

     An earlier pass shrank padding and squeezed the product images to make the
     page shorter. Wrong lever: a shorter page made of tighter boxes is a worse
     page. Height comes off by removing things instead — the hero loses its
     chip and its secondary link, the certifications became a badge row — and
     what's left gets MORE space, not less. */
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-sec{padding:var(--sp-8) 0}}
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-head{margin-bottom:var(--sp-7)}}
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-q summary{padding:var(--sp-5)}}

  /* One clear action in the hero. The chip repeated the marquee above it and
     the second link competed with the button for the same glance — the whole
     complaint was not knowing where to go. */
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-eyebrow{display:none}}
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-mid .gh-cta2{display:none}}
  /* Copy to the top of the fold, figures along the bottom — two bands instead
     of one crowded middle. Centring the copy put it right where the Chums stand,
     which is why the clearance kept coming out at 5-13px and why every fix so
     far has been a choice between smaller words and smaller characters. Stacked
     this way the copy (~330px) and the figures (~257px) both fit the ~650px of
     hero that's visible before you scroll, with real air between them, and the
     Chums can come back IN from the edges instead of being pushed off. */
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-pin{align-content:start;padding-top:7vh;padding-bottom:0}}
  /* On a phone the sizes stop being the issue and the BLOCKS become it: the
     wordmark is ~92px tall while three lines of 38px headline is ~118px, so the
     headline out-weighed the logo even though its letters were smaller. The
     logo grows, the headline drops another step to --fs-2xl and falls to two
     lines, and the logo is comfortably the largest thing again. */
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-logo{width:min(360px,76vw)}}
  @media(max-width:749px){.gh-mid .gh-tag{font-size:var(--fs-2xl)}}

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
    .gh-stage{height:auto}.gh-pin{position:relative;height:auto;padding:70px 0}
    .gh-pin .gh-gummy{display:none}.gh-mid{opacity:1!important}.gh-reveal{display:none}
    .gh-chum{animation:none;opacity:1}
    .gh-g{animation:none}
  }
</style>

